<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="ws_8_2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>8.2 Practice Worksheet</title>



<exercise>
<introduction>
<p>
Determine the radian measure of each angle and in which quadrant the terminal side of the angle is located.
</p>
</introduction>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>12^\circ</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Multiply by <m>\frac{\pi}{180}</m> to convert:
<md>
12^\circ  \cdot \frac{\pi}{180}  \quad = \quad \frac{12\pi}{180} \quad = \quad \frac{\pi}{15} \rad .
</md> This is in quadrant 1.
</p>
</solution>
</task>



<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>450^\circ</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Multiply by <m>\frac{\pi}{180}</m> to convert:
<md>
450^\circ  \cdot \frac{\pi}{180} \quad = \quad \frac{450\pi }{180}  \quad = \quad \frac{5\pi}{2} \rad.
</md> Note that <m>\frac{5\pi}{2}</m> radians is coterminal to <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m> radians which is <m>90^\circ </m>, so this angle is on the positive <m>y</m>-axis.
</p>
</solution>
</task>



<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>610^\circ</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Multiply by <m>\frac{\pi}{180}</m> to convert:
<md>
610^\circ  \cdot \frac{\pi}{180} \quad = \quad \frac{610\pi }{180}  \quad = \quad \frac{61\pi }{18} \rad.
</md> This is in quadrant 3, because it is coterminal to <m>250^\circ </m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>



<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>-275^\circ</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Multiply by <m>\frac{\pi}{180}</m> to convert:
<md>
-275^\circ \cdot \frac{\pi}{180} \quad = \quad \frac{-275\pi }{180}  \quad = \quad \frac{-55\pi }{36} \rad.
</md> This is in quadrant 1 because it is coterminal to <m>85^\circ </m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>



<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>-815^\circ</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Multiply by <m>\frac{\pi}{180}</m> to convert:
<md>
-815^\circ  \cdot \frac{\pi}{180} \quad = \quad \frac{-815\pi }{180}  \quad = \quad \frac{-163\pi }{36} \rad.
</md> This is in quadrant 3 because it is coterminal to <m>265^\circ </m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>


</exercise>



<exercise>
<introduction>
<p>
Determine the degree measure of each radian angle and in which quadrant the terminal side of the angle is located.
</p>
</introduction>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>7\pi</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Multiply by <m>\frac{180}{\pi }</m> to convert:
<md>
7\pi \cdot \frac{180}{\pi } \quad = \quad 1260^\circ .
</md> This is on the negative <m>x</m>-axis because it is coterminal to <m>180^\circ </m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>



<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>\dfrac{\pi}{30}</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Multiply by <m>\frac{180}{\pi }</m> to convert:
<md>
\frac{\pi}{30} \cdot \frac{180}{\pi } \quad = \quad \frac{180}{30} \quad = \quad 6^\circ .
</md> This is in quadrant 1.
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>-2\pi</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Multiply by <m>\frac{180}{\pi }</m> to convert:
<md>
-2\pi \cdot \frac{180}{\pi } \quad = \quad -360^\circ .
</md> This is on the positive <m>x</m>-axis because it is coterminal to <m>0^\circ </m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>\dfrac{-35\pi}{6}</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Multiply by <m>\frac{180}{\pi }</m> to convert:
<md>
-\frac{35\pi}{6}\cdot \frac{180}{\pi } \quad = \quad \frac{-6300}{6} \quad = \quad -1050^\circ  .
</md> This is in quadrant 1 because it is coterminal to <m>30^\circ </m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>4</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Multiply by <m>\frac{180}{\pi }</m> to convert:
<md>
4 \cdot \frac{180}{\pi } \quad = \quad \frac{720}{\pi } \quad \approx  \quad 229^\circ .
</md> This is in quadrant 3.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

</exercise>



<exercise>
<introduction>
<p>
Find the arc length spanned by each angle on a circle of radius <m>3</m> feet. Angles are in radians unless specifically marked as degrees.
</p>
</introduction>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>45^\circ</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
First, convert <m>45^\circ </m> to radians, which is <m>\frac{\pi}{4}</m> radians. The arc length is then
<md>
S = \frac{\pi}{4}\cdot 3 = \frac{3\pi}{4} \text{ feet} .
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>\dfrac{3\pi}{4}</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
As the angle is already in radians, we can just compute the arc length directly:
<md>
S = \frac{3\pi}{4}\cdot 3 = \frac{9\pi}{4} \text{ feet} .
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>2\pi</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
As the angle is already in radians, we can just compute the arc length directly:
<md>
S = 2\pi \cdot 3 = 6\pi \text{ feet} .
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>360^\circ</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Because <m>360^\circ  = 2\pi</m> radians, then the arc length is
<md>
S = 2\pi \cdot 3 = 6\pi \text{ feet} .
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>\dfrac{\pi}{4}</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The angle is already in radians, so
<md>
S = \frac{\pi}{4}\cdot 3 = \frac{3\pi}{4} \text{ feet} .
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>


</exercise>



<exercise>
<introduction>
<p>
What angle corresponds to the given number of rotations about the unit circle from the positive <m>x</m>-axis? Give both the radian and degree measure.
</p>
</introduction>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
2 counterclockwise
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
This corresponds to <m>720^\circ  = 4\pi </m> radians.
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
3 clockwise
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
This corresponds to <m>-1080^\circ  = -6\pi </m> radians.
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
1 clockwise
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
This corresponds to <m>-360^\circ  = -2\pi </m> radians.
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
1/4 clockwise
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
This corresponds to <m>-90^\circ  = -\frac{\pi}{2}</m> radians.
</p>
</solution>
</task>


</exercise>






</worksheet>